> On Jun 13, 2019, at 9:06 AM, Ben Teaches English Overseas =
<ben@benteachesenglishoverseas.com> wrote:
>=20
> Hi Chibuzor,=20
>=20
> I've gone over your resume in detail. Please understand that this =
critique is written with your applying to ESL positions overseas in =
mind. Feel free to implement what you like and disregard what you don't. =
It's of course ultimately your document.=20
>=20
> That said, you have hired me to do this review and give you unbiased =
and direct feedback, so I will not hold back in any area I think you can =
improve. =20
>=20
> When I first open a resume, I like 5 things to jump out at me =
immediately before scrolling down. These are:=20
>=20
> 1.) Native level speaker=20
> 2.) Degrees
> 3.) Certs=20
> 4.) Skype
> 5.) Availability=20
>=20
> These are the five pieces of data that most everyone looking through =
resumes is seeking.  According to Statistic Brain, the human attention =
span is 8 seconds, which is 1 second shorter than a goldfish, so it's =
especially important that this information be easy to read.=20
>=20
>=20
>=20
> Starting from the top, I would:=20
>=20
> 1.) put USA after WV
>=20
> 2.) put a +1 in front of my phone number=20
>=20
> 3.) consider listing a mailing address=20
>=20
>=20
>=20
> These three items above lend credibility and remind the reader subtly =
that you have a home in the US and you are an American and native =
speaker.=20
>=20
>=20
> I am a big proponent of creating summary statements that immediately =
grab the attention of the reader. I like to let them know within 2 =
seconds of opening our document why they need to give us the interview. =
Instead of telling the reader what we're looking for, I like to show =
what we have to offer. I'd consider creating a bold statement between =
your contact details and education sections that shows what you bring to =
the table. Based on what I'm reading here, it might be something like:=20=

>=20
>=20
>=20
> TEFL-Certified Professional Educator with Bachelor's Degree and =
American Passport Available Immediately=20
>=20
>=20
>=20
>=20
> ^^If I were looking for a qualified teacher and read this upon opening =
your resume, you'd absolutely have my attention. You show right off the =
bat that you check all the boxes of the average employer abroad.=20
>=20
>=20
> Education section:=20
>=20
> I'd list USA after WV. Many people are unfamiliar with US cities and =
states. It's totally up to you if you want to list graduation dates, but =
I would not here. It will bring attention to the fact that you're a =
recent graduate. I wouldn't deny it, but I just wouldn't highlight it. I =
would drop the GPA as well.=20
>=20
>=20
>=20
> Professional Experience:=20
>=20
> I would put USA after all of these city and state combos.=20
>=20
>=20
>=20
> My honest feeling is that we should seriously consider dropping the =
jobs that have really short durations. I have been beaten up in =
interviews so many times because of 3-6 month jobs that I have deleted =
them from my resume over the years. Why?=20
>=20
> Employers are trained to look for red flags just like we look for red =
flags with them. They look for a few things in ESL:=20
>=20
> 1.) Very short duration employment. This could potentially show you're =
a flight risk. Many people list every short term position they've ever =
had and employers say "They are going to leave after 3 months. Too =
risky. We don't want to invest in the visa and getting him over here if =
there's a high likelihood he won't last." They often gloss over these =
too... and I'd hate for them to dismiss your resume without realizing =
what were you doing at all these jobs back to back.=20
>=20
>=20
> 2.) Listing a lot of intern and tutoring jobs. Many people pad their =
resumes with this because it's often unverifiable experience. I have =
dropped this from my resume because I got tired of explaining =
myself--just like with short duration employment. I'm sure countless =
others scrapped my resume without allowing me to explain.=20
>=20
>=20
> 3.) Frequently changing locations. This brings about concern for =
obvious reasons.=20
>=20
>=20
>=20
> Your Evoqua experience is solid. It's a year and a half in duration =
and it's obvious you did a lot. If you had any experience training, =
instructing, working with people whose native language was not English, =
etc... I would go into that in more detail.=20
>=20
>=20
>=20
> The one time I would seriously consider keeping this intern experience =
in here is if I were applying to specific Safety English programs like =
with Saudi Aramco or something similar. In that case, it could be useful =
to show you've had the training.=20
>=20
>=20
> Research Experience:=20
>=20
> This looks great.=20
>=20
> Skills:=20
>=20
> It's up to you if you want to leave this on here or not. If you're =
planning to teach computer stuff, this could be useful. For ESL, I think =
honestly that most people won't know these programs and acronyms.=20
>=20
>=20
>=20
> Your activities look great. The only thing to point out is that under =
Mountaineer United, you begin to use punctuation in your final two =
bullets. I would delete it so that these match the format of the rest of =
your resume.=20
>=20
>=20
> Next step:=20
>=20
> I think with a few changes implemented, you'll be happy with how this =
turns out. I've attached some design options to this email to help =
conceptualize how the drafts will look. I have your photo (which is =
great by the way) so all I will need is our rough draft once you are =
ready and your design choice to get these started.=20
>=20
>=20
> Please ask any and all questions you may have and feel free to bounce =
any ideas off me. I'm always here to help.=20
>=20
>=20
> I hope your week's gone great so far!
>=20
> Ben=20
>=20
