-webkit-nbsp-mode: space; line-break: after-white-space;" class=3D""><div =
class=3D""><div></div><div><br class=3D""></div><div><br =
class=3D""><blockquote type=3D"cite" class=3D""><div class=3D"">On Jun =
13, 2019, at 9:06 AM, Ben Teaches English Overseas &lt;<a =
href=3D"mailto:ben@benteachesenglishoverseas.com" =
class=3D"">ben@benteachesenglishoverseas.com</a>&gt; wrote:</div><br =
class=3D"Apple-interchange-newline"><div class=3D""><div dir=3D"ltr" =
class=3D"">Hi Chibuzor, <br class=3D""><br class=3D"">I've gone over =
your resume in detail. Please understand that this critique is written =
with your applying to ESL positions overseas in mind. Feel free to =
implement what you like and disregard what you don't. It's of course =
ultimately your document. <br class=3D""><br class=3D"">That said, you =
have hired me to do this review and give you unbiased and direct =
feedback, so I will not hold back in any area I think you can improve. =
&nbsp;<br class=3D""><br class=3D"">When I first open a resume, I like 5 =
things to jump out at me immediately before scrolling down. These are: =
<br class=3D""><br class=3D"">1.) Native level speaker <br class=3D"">2.) =
Degrees<br class=3D"">3.) Certs <br class=3D"">4.) Skype<br class=3D"">5.)=
 Availability <br class=3D""><br class=3D"">These are the five pieces of =
data that most everyone looking through resumes is seeking.&nbsp; =
According to Statistic Brain, the human attention span is 8 seconds, =
which is 1 second shorter than a goldfish, so it's especially important =
that this information be easy to read.&nbsp;<div class=3D""><br =
class=3D""></div><div class=3D""><br class=3D""><br class=3D"">Starting =
from the top, I would: <br class=3D""><br class=3D"">1.) put USA after =
WV<br class=3D""><br class=3D"">2.) put a +1 in front of my phone number =
<br class=3D""><br class=3D"">3.) consider listing a mailing address <br =
class=3D""><br class=3D""><br class=3D""><br class=3D"">These three =
items above lend credibility and remind the reader subtly that you have =
a home in the US and you are an American and native speaker. <br =
class=3D""><br class=3D""><br class=3D"">I am a big proponent of =
creating summary statements that immediately grab the attention of the =
reader. I like to let them know within 2 seconds of opening our document =
why they need to give us the interview. Instead of telling the reader =
what we're looking for, I like to show what we have to offer. I'd =
consider creating a bold statement between your contact details and =
education sections that shows what you bring to the table. Based on what =
I'm reading here, it might be something like:&nbsp;</div><div =
class=3D""><br class=3D""></div><div class=3D""><br class=3D""></div><div =
class=3D""><b class=3D""><br class=3D"">TEFL-Certified Professional =
Educator with Bachelor's Degree and American Passport Available =
Immediately&nbsp;</b></div><div class=3D""><br class=3D""></div><div =
class=3D""><br class=3D""></div><div class=3D""><br class=3D""></div><div =
class=3D""><br class=3D"">^^If I were looking for a qualified teacher =
and read this upon opening your resume, you'd absolutely have my =
attention. You show right off the bat that you check all the boxes of =
the average employer abroad. <br class=3D""><br class=3D""><br =
class=3D"">Education section: <br class=3D""><br class=3D"">I'd list USA =
after WV. Many people are unfamiliar with US cities and states. It's =
totally up to you if you want to list graduation dates, but I would not =
here. It will bring attention to the fact that you're a recent graduate. =
I wouldn't deny it, but I just wouldn't highlight it. I would drop the =
GPA as well. <br class=3D""><br class=3D""><br class=3D""><br =
class=3D"">Professional Experience: <br class=3D""><br class=3D"">I =
would put USA after all of these city and state combos.&nbsp;</div><div =
class=3D""><br class=3D""></div><div class=3D""><br class=3D""><br =
class=3D"">My honest feeling is that we should seriously consider =
dropping the jobs that have really short durations. I have been beaten =
up in interviews so many times because of 3-6 month jobs that I have =
deleted them from my resume over the years. Why? <br class=3D""><br =
class=3D"">Employers are trained to look for red flags just like we look =
for red flags with them. They look for a few things in ESL: <br =
class=3D""><br class=3D"">1.) Very short duration employment. This could =
potentially show you're a flight risk. Many people list every short term =
position they've ever had and employers say "They are going to leave =
after 3 months. Too risky. We don't want to invest in the visa and =
getting him over here if there's a high likelihood he won't last." They =
often gloss over these too... and I'd hate for them to dismiss your =
resume without realizing what were you doing at all these jobs back to =
back.&nbsp;</div><div class=3D""><br class=3D""><br class=3D"">2.) =
Listing a lot of intern and tutoring jobs. Many people pad their resumes =
with this because it's often unverifiable experience. I have dropped =
this from my resume because I got tired of explaining myself--just like =
with short duration employment. I'm sure countless others scrapped my =
resume without allowing me to explain.&nbsp;<br class=3D""><br =
class=3D""><br class=3D"">3.) Frequently changing locations. This brings =
about concern for obvious reasons. <br class=3D""><br class=3D""><br =
class=3D""><br class=3D"">Your Evoqua experience is solid. It's a year =
and a half in duration and it's obvious you did a lot. If you had any =
experience training, instructing, working with people whose native =
language was not English, etc... I would go into that in more detail. =
<br class=3D""><br class=3D""><br class=3D""><br class=3D"">The one time =
I would seriously consider keeping this intern experience in here is if =
I were applying to specific Safety English programs like with Saudi =
Aramco or something similar. In that case, it could be useful to show =
you've had the training. <br class=3D""><br class=3D""><br =
class=3D"">Research Experience: <br class=3D""><br class=3D"">This looks =
great. <br class=3D""><br class=3D"">Skills: <br class=3D""><br =
class=3D"">It's up to you if you want to leave this on here or not. If =
you're planning to teach computer stuff, this could be useful. For ESL, =
I think honestly that most people won't know these programs and =
acronyms. <br class=3D""><br class=3D""><br class=3D""><br class=3D"">Your=
 activities look great. The only thing to point out is that under =
Mountaineer United, you begin to use punctuation in your final two =
bullets. I would delete it so that these match the format of the rest of =
your resume. <br class=3D""><br class=3D""><br class=3D"">Next =
step:&nbsp;</div><div class=3D""><br class=3D"">I think with a few =
changes implemented, you'll be happy with how this turns out. I've =
attached some design options to this email to help conceptualize how the =
drafts will look. I have your photo (which is great by the way) so all I =
will need is our rough draft once you are ready and your design choice =
to get these started. <br class=3D""><br class=3D""><br class=3D"">Please =
ask any and all questions you may have and feel free to bounce any ideas =
